## The Repository Pattern (in a nutshell)

| What it is | Why we use it |
|------------|---------------|
| **An abstraction layer** between the *business/domain* code and the *data store* (SQL, NoSQL, file, web‑service, …). | **Decouple** your domain logic from persistence technology. |
| **It behaves like an in‑memory collection** of domain objects (e.g. `ICollection<T>`). | **Make the data layer testable** – you can inject a fake repository in unit tests. |
| Typically paired with **Unit of Work** (to batch changes). | **Encourage a clear separation of concerns** – repository = “how to get/save”, service = “what to do with data”. |

> *Short definition*: A repository mediates between the domain and data mapping layers, providing a collection‑like interface for accessing domain objects.

---

## Typical responsibilities

| Responsibility | Repository code |
|----------------|-----------------|
| **Query** objects | `GetById`, `GetAll`, `Find`, `Where` |
| **Persist** objects | `Add`, `Update`, `Delete` |
| **Track** changes | Usually handled by a unit‑of‑work or the underlying ORM (e.g., `DbContext`) |
| **Hide** persistence details | No direct `SqlConnection`/`HttpClient` calls in the domain code |

---

## Example in C# (EF Core)

Below is a minimal, compile‑ready example that shows:

1. Domain entities (`Product`).
2. A repository interface (`IProductRepository`).
3. A concrete implementation (`ProductRepository`) that uses EF Core.
4. A service that consumes the repository.
5. A unit test that injects a fake repository.

> **Assumptions**  
> * .NET 6/7, EF Core 7  
> * In‑memory database for tests

### 1. Domain

```csharp
namespace Domain
{
    public class Product
    {
        public int Id { get; set; }          // PK
        public string Name { get; set; }     // required
        public decimal Price { get; set; }   // required
    }
}
```

### 2. Repository interface

```csharp
namespace Infrastructure.Repositories
{
    using Domain;
    using System.Linq;

    public interface IProductRepository : IDisposable
    {
        // Query
        Task<Product?> GetByIdAsync(int id, CancellationToken ct = default);
        IQueryable<Product> GetAll();           // IQueryable for further filtering
        IQueryable<Product> Find(Func<Product, bool> predicate);

        // Commands
        Task AddAsync(Product product, CancellationToken ct = default);
        Task UpdateAsync(Product product, CancellationToken ct = default);
        Task DeleteAsync(int id, CancellationToken ct = default);

        // Persist
        Task<int> SaveChangesAsync(CancellationToken ct = default);
    }
}
```

### 3. Concrete implementation (EF Core)

```csharp
using Domain;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using System;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace Infrastructure.Repositories
{
    public class AppDbContext : DbContext
    {
        public AppDbContext(DbContextOptions<AppDbContext> options)
            : base(options) { }

        public DbSet<Product> Products => Set<Product>();

        protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
        {
            modelBuilder.Entity<Product>(e =>
            {
                e.HasKey(p => p.Id);
                e.Property(p => p.Name).IsRequired();
                e.Property(p => p.Price).HasColumnType("decimal(18,2)");
            });
        }
    }

    public class ProductRepository : IProductRepository
    {
        private readonly AppDbContext _context;

        public ProductRepository(AppDbContext context) => _context = context;

        public IQueryable<Product> GetAll() => _context.Products;

        public IQueryable<Product> Find(Func<Product, bool> predicate) =>
            _context.Products.Where(predicate).AsQueryable();

        public async Task<Product?> GetByIdAsync(int id, CancellationToken ct = default) =>
            await _context.Products.FindAsync(new object[] { id }, ct);

        public async Task AddAsync(Product product, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            await _context.Products.AddAsync(product, ct);
        }

        public async Task UpdateAsync(Product product, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            _context.Products.Update(product);
            await Task.CompletedTask; // nothing async here
        }

        public async Task DeleteAsync(int id, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            var entity = await GetByIdAsync(id, ct);
            if (entity is not null)
                _context.Products.Remove(entity);
        }

        public async Task<int> SaveChangesAsync(CancellationToken ct = default) =>
            await _context.SaveChangesAsync(ct);

        public void Dispose() => _context.Dispose();
    }
}
```

> **Why async?**  
> Even though `Add` and `Update` are synchronous on EF Core, marking them async keeps the API consistent and future‑proofs against async persistence (e.g., async file I/O).

### 4. Business service

```csharp
using Domain;
using Infrastructure.Repositories;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace Application
{
    public class ProductService
    {
        private readonly IProductRepository _repo;

        public ProductService(IProductRepository repo) => _repo = repo;

        public async Task<IReadOnlyList<Product>> GetAllProductsAsync(CancellationToken ct = default)
            => await _repo.GetAll().ToListAsync(ct);

        public async Task<Product?> GetProductAsync(int id, CancellationToken ct = default)
            => await _repo.GetByIdAsync(id, ct);

        public async Task<Product> CreateAsync(Product dto, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            await _repo.AddAsync(dto, ct);
            await _repo.SaveChangesAsync(ct);
            return dto;
        }

        public async Task UpdateAsync(Product dto, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            await _repo.UpdateAsync(dto, ct);
            await _repo.SaveChangesAsync(ct);
        }

        public async Task DeleteAsync(int id, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            await _repo.DeleteAsync(id, ct);
            await _repo.SaveChangesAsync(ct);
        }
    }
}
```

### 5. Registering in DI (Program.cs)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(options =>
    options.UseInMemoryDatabase("DemoDb")); // or UseSqlServer(...)

builder.Services.AddScoped<IProductRepository, ProductRepository>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<ProductService>();
```

### 6. Unit test – mocking the repository

```csharp
using Domain;
using Infrastructure.Repositories;
using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;
using Moq;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Collections.Generic;

[TestClass]
public class ProductServiceTests
{
    [TestMethod]
    public async Task CreateAsync_ShouldAddProduct()
    {
        // Arrange
        var mockRepo = new Mock<IProductRepository>();
        var product = new Product { Id = 1, Name = "Foo", Price = 9.99M };

        mockRepo.Setup(r => r.AddAsync(product, It.IsAny<CancellationToken>()))
                .Returns(Task.CompletedTask);
        mockRepo.Setup(r => r.SaveChangesAsync(It.IsAny<CancellationToken>()))
                .ReturnsAsync(1);

        var service = new ProductService(mockRepo.Object);

        // Act
        var result = await service.CreateAsync(product);

        // Assert
        Assert.AreEqual(product, result);
        mockRepo.Verify(r => r.AddAsync(product, It.IsAny<CancellationToken>()), Times.Once);
        mockRepo.Verify(r => r.SaveChangesAsync(It.IsAny<CancellationToken>()), Times.Once);
    }
}
```

